# fixture profile level: leaf profile (multi-parent: ../base and
# ../arch/amd64, in that order -- see ./parent).
# Exercises incremental removal ("-bar") across profile levels: "bar" was
# added by ../base and must be gone from the final set, while "foo" (also
# from ../base) survives untouched.
# "-cancelme" is a no-op here (no other fixture source ever adds
# "cancelme" to the global USE accumulation) -- it exercises a *different*
# thing entirely: dev-libs/cancelledpkg's own IUSE default ("+cancelme")
# gets genuinely cancelled by this profile-level "-cancelme", proving
# use_tokens (the ordered raw USE= values) are replayed on top of a
# package's own IUSE-defaults seed, not just union-ed as an already-
# flattened set that could only ever add.
# "-repoweakflag": the "Config depth" slice's repo-vs-defaults ordering
# check -- dev-libs/repouseweakpkg's repo-level package.use enables it,
# and this profile-level make.defaults (real configdict[defaults], a
# stronger USE_ORDER layer than configdict[repo]) must win, leaving it
# OFF.
# "-interleaveflag": the per-level defaults-walk check -- ../base's own
# package.use enables it for dev-libs/interleavepkg, and this leaf
# profile's make.defaults (applied *after* base's package.use, one
# profile at a time) cancels it. A flat "all make.defaults then all
# package.use" model would leave it ON.
USE="-bar baz -cancelme -repoweakflag -interleaveflag"
